Justify exchanging a limit with an integral or derivative (@math-series-operations: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Turn-a-finite-Taylor-remainder-into-a-justified-series-representation.lingua#r_00495C8F3915BF7C2DBC7E592E") { r_001E30FA67E83674F48C0427F2
Let continuous f_N on [a,b] converge uniformly to f. The preceding result makes f continuous, and the shared Riemann theory makes these functions integrable. Then |∫_a^b f_N−∫_a^b f|≤(b−a)sup_[a,b]|f_N−f|→0. The supremum bound follows from upper/lower integral bounds. Thus integration and this limit can be exchanged under the stated hypotheses. This argument also supplies an explicit integral error certificate.

Pointwise convergence alone can fail dramatically. For integer n≥2 on [0,1], define a triangular spike: f_n(x)=n²x for 0≤x≤1/n; f_n(x)=2n−n²x for 1/n≤x≤2/n; and zero afterward. Each function is continuous and has integral one, from two triangles of total base 2/n and height n. At x=0 every value is zero, and for any fixed x>0 the support eventually lies to its left. Hence f_n→0 pointwise while the integrals remain one. The sequence is not uniformly convergent; its maximum is n.

Uniform convergence of functions alone does not justify differentiating the limit. For f_n(x)=sin(nx)/n, |f_n(x)|≤1/n uniformly, so f_n→0. Yet f_n'(0)=1 for every n, whereas the derivative of the limit is zero. The derivative observes changes at scales invisible to the value bound. No numerical sampling of f_n substitutes for a derivative condition.

Here is a sufficient theorem adequate for our later series. Suppose f_N are continuously differentiable on [a,b], f_N(x_0) converges to c at one point, and f_N' converge uniformly to g. The derivatives are continuous, so g is continuous. By the fundamental theorem, f_N(x)=f_N(x_0)+∫_(x_0)^x f_N'(t)dt. Uniform integral bounds show that f_N converge uniformly to f(x)=c+∫_(x_0)^x g(t)dt. The fundamental theorem then gives f'=g in the interior, with the appropriate one-sided endpoint interpretation. This proof explains both the single-point anchor and the uniform derivative control.

Apply the theorem to G_N(x)=Σ_(n=0 to N)x^n on [−q,q] with 0<q<1. Its derivative terms satisfy |n x^(n−1)|≤n q^(n−1), whose series converges by the polynomial-geometric tail argument. The values at zero are one. Therefore Σ_(n=1 to ∞)n x^(n−1)=1/(1−x)² on the interior. Repeat with the second derivative, bounded by n(n−1)q^(n−2), to obtain Σn(n−1)x^(n−2)=2/(1−x)³. The point x=0 is handled by the polynomials or by choosing a positive q around it. These are justified derivative identities, not formal differentiation of an unexamined infinite expression.

**Work:** prove the uniform integral inequality, calculate both pieces of the spike integral and locate the failed hypothesis. Explain why the sine example does not satisfy uniform derivative convergence. Derive Σn r^n=r/(1−r)² and Σn²r^n=r(1+r)/(1−r)³ for |r|<1. Reuse [Define the integral and distinguish net change from total magnitude](Define-the-integral-and-distinguish-net-change-from-total-magnitude.lingua#r_0AEC763D73DF3124CDBB2E86D1) and [Bound a finite trigonometric calculation before trusting decimals](Bound-a-finite-trigonometric-calculation-before-trusting-decimals.lingua#r_009546FA47C08EFAEFC38F8134); compare the sufficient theorem with [Basic Analysis I by Jiri Lebl](../sources/Basic-Analysis-I-by-Jiri-Lebl.lingua#r_00D018D5107F06EE77E9066592) section 6.2 without claiming all its stronger results have been proved here.

